INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - KITCHEN - MORNING

Flat-pack boxes, none of them made up. A window full of brown water going out.

JUNE, fifties, coat still on, stands looking at a tide table pinned to the wall beside the door. Biro rings around the low waters. The last ring is three weeks old.

PAUL, forties, comes in with a roll of tape and does not take his coat off either.

PAUL
Surveyor's at four.

JUNE
It says four on the letter.

PAUL
It says four on the letter, so he'll come at half three.

June has not moved from the tide table.

PAUL (CONT'D)
It's just a bit of card, June.

JUNE
She was still marking it.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - KITCHEN - LATER

Two piles on the table. Paul's is large and fast. June's is one gravy boat.

PAUL
Keep, tip, or Elspeth.

JUNE
What does Elspeth want with a gravy boat.

PAUL
Elspeth wants everything. It's a personality.

JUNE
You'd know.

PAUL
I would, yeah.

He puts a colander on the tip pile. June takes it off again and puts it back exactly where it was on the shelf.

PAUL (CONT'D)
Right.

JUNE
It lives there.

PAUL
Nothing lives here, June. That's the whole afternoon.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - FRONT ROOM - DAY

A bureau against the wall, drop-front, locked. June tries it. Paul, taping a box, does not look up.

JUNE
Where's the key.

PAUL
There isn't one.

JUNE
There's always been one.

PAUL
Then it's in the sea with the rest of it.

June looks at him a moment too long. He tapes.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - KITCHEN - DAY

June takes down a tea tin from the top shelf, the one with the coronation on it. Prises the lid.

Buttons. A thimble. Two keys on a loop of string.

She stands with them in her palm, listening to Paul taping in the next room.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - FRONT ROOM - DAY

The bureau open. Paul in the doorway with the tape gun hanging at his side.

Inside: no papers to speak of. A plain cardboard box, sealed, with a crematorium label. And under it a contract of sale, signed, dated.

June turns the date to the light.

JUNE
The eleventh.

PAUL
June.

JUNE
She went on the twenty-ninth.

Nothing from Paul.

JUNE (CONT'D)
You sat in that chapel next to me.

PAUL
She asked me to do it while she could still read it.

JUNE
And me?

PAUL
You were three hundred miles away being furious at her.

JUNE
I was three hundred miles away because she told me to be.

PAUL
She told everybody everything. You're the only one who did as she said.

June puts her hand flat on the cardboard box and leaves it there.

INT. ESTUARY HOUSE - FRONT ROOM - CONTINUOUS

PAUL
Surveyor's at four.

JUNE
You said half three.

PAUL
I said half three.

JUNE
Then we've got an hour.

She picks up the box. Paul steps into the doorway.

PAUL
Tide's going out.

JUNE
I know what the tide's doing. There's a bit of card on the wall about it.

PAUL
You don't go out on a falling tide. She said that to us when we were four years old, she said it every summer, she said it the week she stopped saying anything.

JUNE
She said a lot of things.

She goes past him.

EXT. ESTUARY HOUSE - STEPS - DAY

Stone steps, green at the bottom. June down them with the box under one arm, shoes off, into the mud.

The rowing boat lies over on its side, half a boat length from water.

She puts the box in the boat and starts to push. The mud holds it. She gets nowhere at all.

She pushes again. Nothing.

She stands there with her hands on the transom, breathing.

EXT. MUD FLAT - DAY

Paul comes down the steps in his good shoes and walks into the mud without stopping.

He takes the other side of the transom. They don't look at each other.

PAUL
On three.

JUNE
On three.

PAUL
One.

They both push on two. The boat comes free with a sound like a jar opening.

EXT. ESTUARY - DAY

Brown water, going out fast. Paul on the oars, holding her against the run. June at the stern with the lid off.

JUNE
Here?

PAUL
Anywhere out here. It all goes the same way.

June tips the box. The wind takes some of it back over Paul, over his shoulders, into his hair. He doesn't wipe it off.

JUNE
Sorry.

PAUL
She'd have loved that.

They sit while the boat turns.

PAUL (CONT'D)
We're going to have to walk it back. There's not enough water to row against.

JUNE
Good.

EXT. ESTUARY HOUSE - STEPS - LATE AFTERNOON

The two of them wet to the thigh, sitting on the steps. A car door up on the road. Neither of them stands.

Paul takes the loop of string out of his pocket and works one key off it. Holds it out.

PAUL
Front door.

JUNE
It's sold, Paul.

PAUL
It's sold at four. Half three, apparently.

She takes the key. Puts it in the pocket of the wet coat.

Above them, through the open kitchen door, the tide table on the wall, going nowhere.
